<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Ms. du Pré returns two weeks after her first visit. MRI of the brain and spine three days after that visit showed lesions typical of multiple sclerosis in the brain and the spinal cord, including an enhancing lesion at C3 to C4 that explains her hands, and the lumbar puncture a week ago showed oligoclonal bands in the spinal fluid only. Aquaporin-4 antibody was negative. She was seen today by Dr. Lindgren-Achebe. The numbness and clumsiness in her fingers are no better; she has practiced for only a few minutes at a time this week and has cancelled two more concerts. The electric feeling down her back when she bends her head continues. She is tired by mid-afternoon. She asked what multiple sclerosis will mean for her playing, and whether she can still have children. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Diagnosis</td><td>Relapsing-remitting multiple sclerosis, active: dissemination in space and time on MRI and CSF-specific oligoclonal bands</td></tr><tr><td>Tests since the first visit</td><td>MRI of the brain and cervical and thoracic spine with gadolinium; lumbar puncture; aquaporin-4 antibody; HIV and syphilis serology</td></tr><tr><td>Symptoms</td><td>Numb, clumsy fingers, worse on the left; Lhermitte&apos;s sign; fatigue</td></tr><tr><td>Her questions</td><td>Whether she will play again; whether she can have children; how treatment will fit around concerts and teaching</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

