<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Relapsing-remitting multiple sclerosis, active (G35.A)</content>. Multiple sclerosis by the 1964 McDonald criteria: a current attack (partial cervical myelitis) with an earlier sensory episode in the history, dissemination in space (periventricular, juxtacortical, infratentorial and spinal cord lesions) and in time (enhancing and non-enhancing lesions together), with CSF-specific oligoclonal bands. The 1971 revision is also met, and most lesions show a central vein. Mimics were excluded: vitamin B12 and TSH were normal, aquaporin-4 antibody was negative, and HIV and syphilis serology were negative. MOG antibody was not tested, because nothing suggests MOG antibody disease (no optic neuritis, no long cord lesion, no ADEM-like picture). The relapse limits her hand function and her work, so high-dose steroids are offered. With active disease at 28, Dr. Lindgren-Achebe and she chose early high-efficacy therapy: ocrelizumab, over natalizumab because her JCV antibody is positive (index 1.4) with a higher risk of PML after two years, and over ofatumumab because she prefers two infusions a year to monthly injections when she travels. [S]<list><item>Methylprednisolone 1 g intravenously daily for 3 days from tomorrow; urine dipstick negative today.</item><item>Ocrelizumab 300 mg on day 1 in three weeks and on day 15, then 600 mg every six months.</item><item>PCV20, recombinant zoster dose 1 and hepatitis B dose 1 today, at least two weeks before the first infusion.</item><item>Occupational therapy for hand function and fatigue next week; MS specialist nurse contact.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Vitamin D deficiency (E55.9)</content>. 25-hydroxyvitamin D 16 ng/mL. Replaced to correct the deficiency; it is not a treatment for multiple sclerosis. [S]<list><item>Cholecalciferol 4000 units daily.</item><item>Level in about three months.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Numbness and clumsiness of the hands with Lhermitte&apos;s sign (R20.2)</content>. Explained by the diagnosis made today; superseded. [S]<list><item>Problem resolved.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>MRI of the brain and cervical and thoracic spine with gadolinium</td><td>At diagnosis</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Eleven typical brain lesions in periventricular, juxtacortical and infratentorial sites, one enhancing; enhancing C3 to C4 lesion and non-enhancing T7 lesion; central veins in most lesions</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Cerebrospinal fluid: oligoclonal bands, cell count, protein</td><td>At diagnosis when it adds certainty</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Six CSF-specific oligoclonal bands; IgG index 0.86; white cells 6, protein 42 mg/dL</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Mimics: vitamin B12, TSH, HIV, syphilis</td><td>At diagnosis</td><td>Up to date</td><td>B12 468 pg/mL and TSH 1.6 mIU/L at the first visit; HIV and syphilis negative</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Aquaporin-4 antibody (cell-based assay)</td><td>When a myelitis or optic neuritis raises neuromyelitis optica spectrum disorder</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Negative</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>MOG antibody</td><td>Only when the picture suggests MOG antibody disease</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>No optic neuritis, long cord lesion or ADEM-like picture; typical MS lesions with central veins</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>JCV antibody</td><td>Before choosing natalizumab, and every six months while on it</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Positive, index 1.4 (drawn with the lumbar puncture); natalizumab not chosen</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Hepatitis B (surface antigen, core antibody, surface antibody), varicella and measles immunity</td><td>Before anti-CD20 therapy</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Drawn with the lumbar puncture: hepatitis B surface antigen and core antibody negative, surface antibody negative (not immune); varicella IgG positive; measles immune by two recorded doses</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Immunoglobulins (IgG, IgM)</td><td>At baseline and before each ocrelizumab dose</td><td>Completed today</td><td>IgG 1080 mg/dL, IgM 124 mg/dL</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>25-hydroxyvitamin D</td><td>At diagnosis</td><td>Completed today</td><td>16 ng/mL: deficient</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Pregnancy test</td><td>Before steroids and before each ocrelizumab dose</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Urine pregnancy test negative</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Infection screen before high-dose steroids</td><td>Before each steroid course</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Urine dipstick negative; no fever</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Vaccinations before B-cell therapy</td><td>Inactivated vaccines at least two weeks before the first dose; live vaccines at least four weeks before</td><td>Completed today</td><td>PCV20, recombinant zoster dose 1 and hepatitis B dose 1 given today; no live vaccine needed</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Hand function: nine-hole peg test, both hands</td><td>At diagnosis, after a relapse, and at each review</td><td>Completed today</td><td>24.5 seconds right, 31.0 seconds left (typical for her age about 17 to 19 seconds)</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Neurological examination and EDSS</td><td>At diagnosis and at each review</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Sensory loss in the fingers, mild left finger weakness, Lhermitte&apos;s sign; EDSS 2.0</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Occupational therapy</td><td>For any problem with hand function or fatigue</td><td>Referred</td><td>Assessment booked for next week</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Mood and anxiety (PHQ-9, GAD-7)</td><td>At diagnosis and at each review</td><td>Completed today</td><td>PHQ-9 7 (item 9 scored 0), GAD-7 6</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Pregnancy planning and contraception</td><td>At diagnosis and at each review</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Pregnancy is possible with multiple sclerosis; on ocrelizumab, conception is best planned a few months after a dose, with the neurologist; condoms for now</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Written information and MS specialist nurse</td><td>At diagnosis</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Written information given; nurse contact and relapse line</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>MRI of the brain and spine within one week and a neurology diagnosis within three weeks. Outcome by 1973-03-23: MRI in three days, lumbar puncture in a week, diagnosis and consultation at two weeks [S]</item><item>High-efficacy disease-modifying therapy started within six weeks of diagnosis, after screening and vaccinations. In progress; target 1973-04-27 [S]</item><item>Nine-hole peg times back to within 10 percent of normal in both hands, and full practice resumed. In progress; target 1973-07-13 [S]</item><item>No evidence of disease activity at six months (no relapse, no new or enhancing MRI lesion, no disability progression). In progress; target 1973-10-12 [S]</item><item>25-hydroxyvitamin D of 30 ng/mL or more. In progress; target 1973-07-13 [S]</item><item>PHQ-9 below 10 throughout. In progress; target 1973-10-12 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Immunizations given today: Pneumococcal conjugate vaccine (PCV20), before B-cell therapy; Recombinant zoster vaccine, dose 1 of 2, before B-cell therapy; Hepatitis B vaccine (adjuvanted, two-dose series), dose 1, before B-cell therapy [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Guideline basis: Rae-Grant A, et al. Practice guideline recommendations summary: Disease-modifying therapies for adults with multiple sclerosis. Neurology 2018;90(17):777-788 (PMID 29686116) (2018); Montalban X, et al. ECTRIMS/EAN Guideline on the pharmacological treatment of people with multiple sclerosis. Mult Scler 2018;24(2):96-120 (PMID 29353550) (2018); Multiple sclerosis in adults: management (NICE guideline NG220) (2022); Thompson AJ, et al. Diagnosis of multiple sclerosis: 2017 revisions of the McDonald criteria. Lancet Neurol 2018;17(2):162-173 (PMID 29275977) (2017); Montalban X, et al. Diagnosis of multiple sclerosis: 2024 revisions of the McDonald criteria. Lancet Neurol 2025;24(10):850-865 (doi:10.1016/S1474-4422(25)00270-4) (2025); Wattjes MP, et al. 2021 MAGNIMS-CMSC-NAIMS consensus recommendations on the use of MRI in patients with multiple sclerosis. Lancet Neurol 2021;20(8):653-670 (PMID 34139157) (2021); Farez MF, et al. Practice guideline update summary: Vaccine-preventable infections and immunization in multiple sclerosis. Neurology 2019;93(13):584-594 (PMID 31462584) (2019)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Methylprednisolone from tomorrow for 3 days; occupational therapy next week; ocrelizumab day 1 in three weeks. Review at the second half-dose. Call at once for new weakness, imbalance, loss of vision or bladder control. Next scheduled visit 1973-04-27: second half-dose of the first ocrelizumab course: recovery of hand function, infusion reactions, vaccination, practice and work. [S]</paragraph></text>

